--- a/.codex-research/runs/2026-06-25_20iter_delayed-market-making-tail-characterization/working_notes/claim_ledger.md
+++ b/.codex-research/runs/2026-06-25_20iter_delayed-market-making-tail-characterization/working_notes/claim_ledger.md
@@ -78,6 +78,23 @@
 \]
 Equivalently, an epsilon-comparator version gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\) using quotes \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\). This is a lower bound for a learner not knowing the continuation, not an instance-wise lower bound for a process-aware learner.
 
+**CL-017: Finite same-path two-horizon lower bound.**  
+Status: accepted finite obstruction for horizon-oblivious policies.  
+Consider the deterministic path
+\[
+P_1=\cdots=P_N=0,\qquad P_{N+1}=1/2,\qquad P_{N+2}=1.
+\]
+A policy may know this path and the current time/history, but its first \(N\) actions cannot depend on whether regret will be evaluated at \(T_0=N+1\) or \(T_1=N+2\). With
+\[
+G_0=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^N A_t1\{A_t<1/2\},\qquad
+G_1=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^N A_t1\{A_t<1\},
+\]
+one has \(G_0+G_1\le N\). Using \(\eta\)-comparators \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) to avoid strict-threshold nonattainment,
+\[
+R_{N+1}+R_{N+2}\ge N(1/2-2\eta)-O(1),
+\]
+so \(\max\{R_{N+1},R_{N+2}\}\ge N(1/4-\eta)-O(1)\). This refutes a generic horizon-oblivious oracle collapse, but it is not a raw-tail necessity theorem.
+
 ## Refuted Or Weakened
 
 **CL-006: Original iff conjecture.**  
@@ -119,9 +136,17 @@
 A lower bound should use two processes/classes with identical observations before delayed feedback resolves, but different best fixed quotes. Long tail mass must be payoff-relevant, sufficiently frequent, and unpredictable.
 
 **CL-016: Concatenated block universal lower bound.**  
-Status: promising but not fully formalized.  
+Status: likely formalizable candidate, not yet a proved theorem.  
 Let \(N_i\) be dominant block lengths with \(M_i=\sum_{j<i}(N_j+1)=o(N_i)\), and consider deterministic sequences formed by blocks \(0^{N_i}c_i\), \(c_i\in\{1/2,1\}\). For any process-unaware universal learner, one should be able to choose \(c_i\)'s so that at block endpoints \(T_i=M_i+N_i+1\),
 \[
 R^\pi(T_i)\ge N_i/4-M_i,
 \]
-and hence \(\limsup_i R^\pi(T_i)/T_i\ge1/4\). Remaining work: state whether learners are horizon-aware or anytime, diagonalize if needed, condition on the fixed prior prefix for randomized learners, and write the cross-block pending-reward accounting carefully.
+and hence \(\limsup_i R^\pi(T_i)/T_i\ge1/4\). Iteration 3 reduced the main accounting obstruction: lower-bound the comparator using only the current block, and upper-bound all learner rewards attributable to the previous prefix by \(M_i\). Remaining work: state process-unaware quantifiers, couple randomized learners across the two continuations after the fixed prefix, handle horizon-indexed learner families separately, and write the induction formally. Each sequence in the block class has \(\beta(h)=1\) and \(\gamma(h)\ge1/4\) for all \(h\), but the theorem would be class-uniform/minimax only.
+
+**CL-018: Concatenated same-path horizon-oblivious lower bound.**  
+Status: promising but unproved infinite-process candidate.  
+Concatenate deterministic blocks \(0^{N_i},1/2,1\) with previous length \(M_i=o(N_i)\). For any process-aware but horizon-oblivious anytime policy, the finite two-horizon obstruction CL-017 should imply
+\[
+\max\{R_{T_i^0},R_{T_i^1}\}\ge N_i/4-o(N_i),
+\]
+where \(T_i^0\) and \(T_i^1\) are the two endpoints inside block \(i\). This would show that knowing the whole process law/path is not enough if the evaluation horizon is not an input. It still would not prove \(\beta\)-necessity, because CL-005 gives an anytime learner with \(O(1)\) regret and \(\beta\equiv1\).

