 The main unresolved issue is quantifiers. A true necessity theorem must specify whether "possible" means instance-wise existence of a process-aware learner, a horizon-oblivious process-aware learner, a universal learner over a process class, a minimax/distribution-free guarantee, or a post-history conditional guarantee. The best current lower-bound route is class-uniform/minimax: in a finite block \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner suffers regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation because the two continuations are indistinguishable before the terminal price. Concatenating dominant blocks may yield a universal-class impossibility, but it is not an instance-wise characterization.
+
+Iteration 3 sharpened two non-instance-wise obstruction routes. For universal/process-unaware learners, the concatenated block proof looks likely formalizable: after a fixed prefix of length \(M_i=o(N_i)\), the current block lower-bounds the comparator while all old learner rewards are capped by \(M_i\). This remains a candidate theorem until the induction, randomized coupling, and horizon-indexed variants are written. Separately, even a process-aware but horizon-oblivious learner can face a same-path prefix conflict on \(0^N,1/2,1\): the prefixes \(N+1\) and \(N+2\) require incompatible early quotes, giving one regret \(\ge N/4-o(N)\). This refutes a generic anytime oracle collapse, but sparse vanishing spikes still refute raw \(\beta\)-necessity in the anytime regime.
